urthermore, each vertex in $V_2$ needs at least two private neighbors in the set labeled 0 ($V_0$), and this implies that $|V| \\\\ge \\\\frac{3}{2} W$. It became apparent that the inequality `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f` holds for all functions, not just minimal ones. I'm trying to figure out where I missed the easy proof.\\n\\n\\n**Exploring alternative proofs**\\n\\nExamining existing lemmas for inspiration, noting the length of proofs for different conditions. The `radius_add_one_le_romanWeight_of_unique_two` proof is lengthy. Considering `radius_add_one_le_romanWeight_of_card_le` and `two_le_romanWeight` for simpler approaches. Investigating a proof strategy for the case with at least two vertices labeled '2'.\\n\\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\\n\\n\\n\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"name\":\"run_command\",\"args\":{\"CommandLine\":\"\\\"python3 -c \\\\\\\"\\\\nimport itertools\\\\nfrom collections import deque\\\\n\\\\ndef bfs_dists(adj, n, v):\\\\n    dist = {v: 0}\\\\n    q = deque([v])\\\\n    while q:\\\\n        curr = q.popleft()\\\\n        for nxt in adj[curr]:\\\\n            if nxt not in dist:\\\\n                dist[nxt] = dist[curr] + 1\\\\n                q.append(nxt)\\\\n    return dist\\\\n\\\\nfor n in [5, 6, 7, 8]:\\\\n    adj = {i: [] for i in range(n)}\\\\n    for i in range(n - 1):\\\\n        adj[i].append(i + 1)\\\\n        adj[i + 1].append(i)\\\\n    # Test an f with multiple 2s\\\\n    # e.g. for P_6: f = (0, 2, 0, 0, 2, 0), W = 4\\\\n    # e.g. for P_8: f = (0, 2, 0, 0, 0, 2, 0, 0), W = 4, but is that valid?\\\\n    # No, for P_8, f must dominate everything: (0, 2, 0, 0, 2, 0, 0, 2) W=6\\\\n    for f in itertools.product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\\\\n        if sum(f) >= n: continue # card <= W is already solved\\\\n        valid = True\\\\n        for v in range(n):\\\\n            if f[v] == 0 and not any(f[u] == 2 for u in adj[v]):\\\\n                valid = False; break\\\\n        if not valid: continue\\\\n        c2 = [v for v in range(n) if f[v] == 2]\\\\n        if len(c2) < 2: continue # unique 2 is already solved\\\\n        W = sum(f)\\\\n        # find w, k\\\\n        found = False\\\\n        for w in range(n):\\\\n            d = bfs_dists(adj, n, w)\\\\n            for k in range(n):\\\\n                compl = sum(1 for u in range(n) if d[u] > k)\\\\n                if k + compl + 1 <= W:\\\\n                    # print(f'P_{n}: W={W}, f={f} -> w={w}, k={k}, compl={compl}, val={k+compl+1}')\\\\n                    found = True\\\\n                    break\\\\n            if found: break\\\\n        assert found, f'NOT FOUND for P_{n}, f={f}'\\\\nprint('Found (w, k) witness for EVERY Roman dominating function with |V_2| >= 2 on P_5..P_8!')\\\\n\\\\\\\"\\\"\",\"Cwd\":\"\\\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\\\"\",\"WaitMsBeforeAsync\":\"10000\",\"toolAction\":\"\\\"Check (w, k) witness for all f with multiple 2s\\\"\",\"toolSummary\":\"\\\"Check (w, k) witness\\\"\"}}]}\r\n{\"step_index\":1570,\"source\":\"MODEL\",\"type\":\"GENERIC\",\"status\":\"DONE\",\"created_at\":\"2026-09-11T23:57:29Z\",\"content\":\"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:57:29+02:00\\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:57:29+02:00\\n\\nThe command exited with code 0.\\nOutput:\\nFound (w, k) witness for EVERY Roman dominating function with |V_2| >= 2 on P_5..P_8!\\r\\n\\n\"}\r\n\n"}
